# iso_cov_no_leak.tkr — the NATIVE-route regressor of E1-C3 hermeticity (0.3.1
# paralelizacao-eixo1-isolamento-por-task §5), twin to `iso_intern_no_leak` on the native backend.
#
# WHAT IT PINS. The function-coverage sink is now a per-task member (E1-C1) that `task_reset()` clears
# (E1-C2), and it is one of the Category C singletons the NATIVE backend lowers directly (`cov_mark`/
# `cov_distinct`). A writer test marks an id, a reader test counts the distinct marks, and the two run
# in BOTH orders. Because each test enters under `task_reset()`, the reader counts ZERO in either
# order — the count does not depend on which test ran first, which is the hermeticity the axis buys.
#
# TEST = STDOUT, NEVER EXIT (the axis law): the verdict is the token on stdout, not the exit code.
#
# NATIVE by construction: no env is declared, so `When built and run` uses the default (native)
# backend; the C route is covered by the same source through the own-vs-C differential.

Feature: iso_cov_no_leak — a reset at each #test boundary makes the coverage reader hermetic

  Scenario: the coverage reader counts zero in both orders, so the reshuffle exposes no residue
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "iso-cov-no-leak 0 0"
